        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Many chances"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Two coins, two dice, a pack of cards. Listing outcomes in a table makes probability problems easy."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "36 outcomes",
          "52 cards",
          "at least one head",
          "doublet",
          "experimental vs theoretical",
          "sample space",
          "sum of 7",
          "two coins"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "When two coins are tossed, the outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT, four equally likely outcomes.\nP(two heads) = 1/4 and P(at least one head) = 3/4.\nWhen two dice are thrown, there are 36 equally likely outcomes.\nP(sum 7) = 6/36 = 1/6 because the pairs (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1) work.\nP(doublet) = 6/36 = 1/6, and P(sum 12) = 1/36.\nA deck has 52 cards in four suits, 13 per suit, with 12 face cards and 4 aces.\nThe theoretical probability predicts. The experimental probability comes from actual trials. With many trials they come close.\nList the sample space in an orderly table so that you do not miss an outcome."
        }
      },
